---
import TopicPage from '../../../../components/TopicPage.astro';
import Widget from '../../../../components/Widget.astro';

const dsCfg = {
  steps: [
    { math: 'Array / list — ordered shelf', note: 'Items in a row, numbered from 0. Instant access to any position (random access); inserting in the middle means shuffling everything after it.' },
    { math: 'Hash table — the phone book', note: 'A key hashed into a slot: average instant lookup by name, not position. The workhorse behind dictionaries, caches, and database indexes.' },
    { math: 'Stack — last in, first out', note: 'Push and pop from one end. The "undo" pile, and the mechanism tracking function calls in every running program.' },
    { math: 'Queue — first in, first out', note: 'The fair line. Print jobs, message systems, and breadth-first search all run on queues.' },
    { math: 'Tree — hierarchy with fast search', note: 'A branching structure: file systems, JSON, and binary search trees that find items in log n steps. Balanced trees (AVL, B-tree) keep them fast even under abuse.' },
  ],
};

const dsQuiz = {
  questions: [
    {
      q: "A hash table (separate chaining) holds 1,000 entries in 4,000 slots with uniform hashing. Roughly how many comparisons does a FAILED lookup take on average?",
      choices: [
        "About 1 — chain length tracks the load factor, not the table size",
        "About 250 — a quarter of the entries",
        "About 1,000 — the whole table",
        "Impossible to say without the key",
      ],
      answer: 0,
      why: "The load factor α = 1000/4000 = 0.25, and a failed chained search inspects about α ≈ 0.25 expected entries — near-instant. Key insight: hash-table performance tracks the load factor, not the table's size, which is exactly why tables resize.",
    },
    {
      q: "You insert 1, 2, 3, 4, ..., 10,000 into a plain unbalanced binary search tree. How does search behave?",
      choices: [
        "O(log n) — trees are always logarithmic",
        "O(1) — small numbers hash well",
        "O(n) — the tree degenerates into a linked list down the right spine",
        "O(n log n)",
      ],
      answer: 2,
      why: "A BST's shape depends on insertion order; sorted input builds a 10,000-node staircase with depth 10,000. Self-balancing trees (AVL, red-black) exist precisely to prevent this — rotations keep depth O(log n) under any insertion order.",
    },
    {
      q: "An LRU (least-recently-used) cache must answer 'is x cached?' in O(1) AND evict the oldest entry in O(1). Which composition works?",
      choices: [
        "A hash table alone",
        "A sorted array alone",
        "A hash table mapping keys to nodes of a doubly linked list",
        "Two hash tables",
      ],
      answer: 2,
      why: "The hash table finds the entry instantly; the linked list orders entries by recency, so moving a node and popping the tail are pointer surgery — O(1). Neither structure suffices alone; the skill is composing them.",
    },
  ],
};
---
<TopicPage slug="subjects/computer-science/advanced/data-structures">
  <h2>The idea</h2>
  <p>
    The intermediate lessons taught you arrays and files; the algorithms lesson taught you that operations
    have growth rates. Data structures join the two: <strong>a structure is a container plus a trade-off</strong>
    — it makes some operations constant-time and pays for it by making others expensive. Choosing one is
    choosing which question must be fast. The exam classic, "array or hash table?", is really "random access
    by position, or lookup by name?" — and once you can name the access pattern (what gets looked up, how
    often, in what order), the right structure usually picks itself. This lesson works each container to
    expert depth: the mechanism, the costs with real numbers, and the failure modes.
  </p>

  <Widget no="1" caption="the five containers everything else is built from" widget="equationStep" config={dsCfg} />

  <h2>Arrays: the memory model everything sits on</h2>
  <p>
    An array is a contiguous block: element i lives at address start + i × element-size, which is why
    indexing is a single arithmetic act — <strong>O(1) random access</strong> is not a data-structure trick
    but a consequence of physics (fixed-width slots, arithmetic addresses). The costs follow the same
    logic: inserting at the front shifts every element — O(n) — and growth beyond the allocation requires
    a new block and a copy (dynamic arrays amortise this by doubling: 1 copy, then 2, then 4... so n
    appends cost O(n) total, roughly 2 elements copied per append on average). Sorted arrays buy
    <strong>binary search</strong>: 1 million items found in about 20 comparisons (log₂ 1,000,000 ≈ 20),
    1 billion in 30 — the logarithm's superpower, at the price of O(n) insertion. This is the syllabus's
    array topic at full depth: know both the access arithmetic and the amortisation story, because every
    other structure is built by escaping one of the array's two costs.
  </p>

  <h2>Hash tables: O(1) by arithmetic, with physics of their own</h2>
  <p>
    A hash table maps keys to slots through a <strong>hash function</strong> — a deterministic scramble
    (a good one avalanche-changes with every input bit) — so "find by name" becomes "compute the slot."
    Two engineering problems define quality. <strong>Collisions</strong>: two keys landing in one slot,
    solved by chaining (each slot holds a small list) or open addressing (probe the next slots). With
    uniform hashing the expected chain length is the <strong>load factor</strong> α = entries ÷ slots:
    keep α under 0.75 and lookups inspect about 1–2 slots regardless of whether the table holds a thousand
    or a billion entries. That is the O(1) average — paid for by <strong>resizing</strong>: when α crosses
    the threshold, rehash everything into a bigger table (amortised O(1), but a single insert pays a lump).
    <strong>Adversarial keys</strong>: if an attacker can choose keys that all collide, the table degrades
    to O(n) per lookup — the hash-flooding denial-of-service attack, which is why languages seed their
    hash functions randomly. The deep point: hash tables are probability-theoretic structures, and their
    guarantees are statistical, not absolute — O(1) <em>expected</em>, O(n) worst case, like the cost table
    says.
  </p>

  <h2>Trees: logarithms as a shape</h2>
  <p>
    A <strong>binary search tree</strong> keeps an ordering invariant — left descendants smaller, right
    larger — so each comparison discards half the remaining tree: search, insert and delete are O(h), where
    h is height. The catch is that h is chosen by insertion order: feed in 1, 2, 3, ..., n and the tree
    becomes a staircase — a linked list with pretensions, O(n) everything. <strong>Self-balancing trees</strong>
    (AVL, red-black) restore logarithmic height with rotations on the way down; red-black trees power most
    standard-library maps, guaranteeing O(log n) worst case <em>and</em> sorted iteration — the two things
    hash tables cannot give. <strong>B-trees</strong> generalise the idea for disks: each node holds dozens
    to hundreds of keys sized to one disk block, so a search reads 3–4 blocks even for billions of items
    (a B-tree of order 500 reaches a billion keys in depth 4) — which is why databases and file systems
    store their indexes this way. The log is the message: depth grows by 1 each time capacity squares.
  </p>
  <p><em>Common misconception:</em> trees are always O(log n). Only <em>balanced</em> trees are; the
  unbalanced BST is O(n) in the worst case, and a heap — the other famous tree — guarantees its O(log n)
  only for insert/extract-min, while lookup of an arbitrary element is O(n). Match the operation list to
  the structure, not the shape to the slogan.</p>

  <h2>Stacks, queues, and the call stack</h2>
  <p>
    The <strong>stack</strong> (LIFO) is the structure behind program execution itself: every function call
    pushes a frame (arguments, locals, return address); every return pops one — which is why infinite
    recursion is a <em>stack overflow</em>, an actual exhausted stack, and why undo history, backtracking
    search and depth-first traversal all fall out of push/pop for free. The <strong>queue</strong> (FIFO)
    is the fairness structure: operating-system job scheduling, message queues between services, printer
    spools — and breadth-first search, which explores a graph in distance layers precisely because a queue
    processes earlier arrivals first. Both are O(1) for their two operations; the design skill is noticing
    which discipline your problem secretly wants (an undo feature is a stack; an inbox is a queue; a
    "most recent, except pinned" feed is neither — now you are doing data-structure design).
  </p>

  <h2>File handling: the persistent tier</h2>
  <p>
    The syllabus's file-handling topic is the same subject at the boundary of the machine: files are the
    <strong>persistent data structures</strong> — they survive power loss, which RAM does not. The design
    choices mirror memory: <strong>sequential files</strong> (records in order; append cheap, lookup means
    reading past everything earlier — the tape model) versus <strong>random-access files</strong> (seek by
    record number — the disk analogue of array indexing); the classic bridge between them is an
    <strong>index file</strong>: a sorted table of key → file position, searched with binary search (again
    the logarithm), then one direct seek — which is, structurally, a B-tree waiting to happen. Serialization
    formats (JSON, CSV, binary) are the schema layer: text is debuggable and portable, binary is compact and
    fast, and the choice is the same trade-off in new clothes. The cognitive habit to keep: when data must
    outlive the program, design the access pattern first and only then pick the file layout.
  </p>

  <h2>Linked lists and heaps: the two structures the table hides</h2>
  <p>
    <strong>Linked lists</strong> are the array's mirror image: nodes holding a value and a pointer, so
    the list can live anywhere in memory. The wins: insertion and removal at a known node are pure
    pointer surgery — O(1) with no shifting, no reallocation (an array must slide everything); size is
    unbounded by contiguous space. The costs: no random access (reaching item i is i pointer hops, O(i));
    every node costs an allocation and a pointer's memory; and the killer in practice —
    <strong>pointer-chasing defeats the CPU cache</strong>, so traversal of 1 million list nodes is
    routinely 10–50× slower than walking an array of the same 1 million values, despite identical big-O.
    Doubly linked lists add a back pointer (O(1) removal given the node — the LRU cache's requirement)
    at the price of two pointers per node. The professional heuristic: prefer arrays for storage,
    lists for splicing — and reach for a list mainly as a building block inside other structures.
  </p>
  <p>
    <strong>Heaps</strong> are the tree that lives in an array: the binary heap stores a complete tree
    in a flat array where the children of index i sit at 2i+1 and 2i+2 (equivalently, the parent of i
    is (i−1)/2) — no pointers at all. The invariant is the heap property (each parent ≤ its children
    for a min-heap), which is deliberately weaker than BST ordering: it promises only that the minimum
    is at the root. Insert appends the value and <strong>sifts up</strong> (swap with the parent while
    smaller): pushing 5, 3, 8, 1 into a min-heap gives [5], then [3,5], then [3,5,8], then sift 1 up
    twice to [1,3,8,5] — log n swaps. Extract-min removes the root, moves the last element to the top
    and <strong>sifts down</strong> — again log n. This is the priority queue of Dijkstra, event
    schedulers and OS run queues; and the same structure, heapified in place, is heapsort — the rare
    sort with O(n log n) worst case and O(1) extra memory (at the cost of poor cache locality, the
    array/tree hybrid's one weakness).
  </p>

  <h2>Graphs as the general case</h2>
  <p>
    The containers so far handle order (arrays), lookup (hashes), hierarchy (trees) and discipline
    (stacks, queues). The <strong>graph</strong> drops every restriction: nodes plus arbitrary
    connections — the general structure, of which lists and trees are special cases (a list is a graph
    where every node has one successor; a tree, where every node has one parent). That generality is why
    the hard modelling questions in industry are graph questions: social networks, road networks,
    dependency graphs (build systems, package managers), state machines, the web itself. Representation
    (adjacency list for sparse real-world graphs, matrix for dense small ones) and the two traversals
    (BFS with a queue for shortest hops, DFS with a stack for structure and cycles) are the gateway
    machinery — developed fully in the graphs-and-DP lesson on this ladder. The design takeaway for this
    lesson: when you cannot fit your problem into a tree or table, stop forcing it; name the entities
    and relationships, and the graph toolkit takes over.
  </p>

  <h2>Choosing under constraint: the worked selection</h2>
  <p>
    Practise the selection discipline on a realistic brief. An app stores a contact list; users scroll it
    in alphabetical order, search it by name, and edit entries constantly. Enumerate the operations and
    frequencies first: display sorted (constant), search by prefix (frequent), insert/update/delete
    (occasional). Now the candidates. A hash table: search O(1), but no ordering — sorted display
    requires a full sort per render, O(n log n) every time. A sorted array: display free, search by
    binary search O(log n), but every insert shifts elements. A balanced tree keyed by name: ordered
    iteration for free, O(log n) search, O(log n) edits — the structure the access pattern wants. The
    follow-through matters as much as the answer: real contact apps sit a tree (or sorted index) beside
    a hash index over the same records, because display and lookup optimise differently — two indexes,
    one store. That is the expert habit in one example: <strong>state the operations with frequencies,
    cost each candidate per operation, and pay only for the operations that actually happen</strong>.
  </p>

  <h2>Going deeper</h2>
  <p>
    <strong>Composition is the expert skill.</strong> Real structures are composites: an LRU cache is a
    hash table whose values are nodes of a doubly linked list — O(1) lookup <em>and</em> O(1) eviction, the
    pair no single classic structure provides; a priority queue is a heap wrapped in an array; a full-text
    search engine is an inverted index (hash from word to posting lists) compressed with trees. When no
    named structure fits, you assemble one from parts whose costs you know. <strong>Probability is
    everywhere</strong>: bloom filters (no false negatives, tunable false-positive rate, 10 bits per element
    for ~1% error) let databases skip disk reads that would certainly miss; skip lists randomise balancing;
    count-min sketches count billions of events in megabytes — the modern extension of hashing into
    "probably correct, certainly fast." <strong>Cache reality</strong>: big-O hides constants that hardware
    does not — a contiguous array walk outruns a pointer-chasing tree by 10–100× at equal complexity
    because of CPU cache lines, which is why high-performance code sometimes prefers "worse" structures in
    better memory layouts. Open problems: can worst-case O(1) hash tables with practical constants be built
    (cuckoo hashing gets close); how should structures behave on non-volatile memory, where the disk/RAM
    boundary the B-tree assumes is dissolving; and what data structures suit append-only, immutable logs —
    the architecture behind modern databases? You now have the vocabulary to read those papers.
  </p>

  <p class="sources"><strong>Sources:</strong> Cormen et al., <em>Introduction to Algorithms</em>; Sedgewick &amp; Wayne, <em>Algorithms</em>, 4th ed.; Knuth, <em>The Art of Computer Programming</em>, vol. 3.</p>

  <Widget no="2" caption="check yourself: load factors, degenerate trees, and composing for O(1)" widget="quiz" config={dsQuiz} />
</TopicPage>
